        </div>
        <dl>
          <dt>What you get</dt><dd>Free student membership tiers with rotating exclusive pricing — commonly $100–150 off laptops during back-to-school season.</dd>
          <dt>How to claim</dt><dd>Verification is usually via SheerID with a school email.</dd>
          <dt>The catch</dt><dd class="catch">Genuinely rotating, so the offer you saw last month may be gone. Worth checking before any large purchase rather than browsing idly.</dd>
        </dl>
        <button class="claimbtn" type="button"><span class="box"></span>Mark claimed</button>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 tally -->
  <section id="tally" class="sec">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">06</span>
      <h2>The honest tally</h2>
      <p class="lede">You have probably seen a version of this list headlined "your student email is worth $5,000." That number is real only if you would otherwise have bought professional licences at list price — and no student was ever going to buy Autodesk at $1,700 a year.</p>
    </div>

    <p>So here it is split into two columns. The left is money that would genuinely have left your account. The right is list value: real, but theoretical.</p>

    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Perk</th><th>Cash you'd actually spend</th><th>List value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Google AI (where eligible)</td><td class="num">$240</td><td class="num">$240</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Microsoft 365</td><td class="num">$100</td><td class="num">$100</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spotify, Prime, YouTube</td><td class="num">$238</td><td class="num">$238</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Notion, Figma, Canva</td><td class="num">$60</td><td class="num">$384</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloud credits</td><td class="num">$40</td><td class="num">$300</td></tr>
          <tr><td>JetBrains</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$289</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Autodesk and MATLAB</td><td class="num">$0</td><td class="num">$2,600</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WSJ, NYT and library databases</td><td class="num">$120</td><td class="num">$900</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><td>Total</td><td class="num">≈ $800 / year</td><td class="num">≈ $5,000 / year</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="k">Why this matters more than the big number</span>
      <p>Roughly $800 a year is the figure worth acting on, and it is still a lot of money for a student. The licence column is worth claiming for a different reason: not because it saves you cash, but because having Fusion 360 or MATLAB or the full JetBrains suite on your machine changes what you can build — and what you can build is what ends up on your resume.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 international -->
  <section id="international" class="sec">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">07</span>
      <h2>If you're outside the U.S.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Nearly every list like this is written for American students and silently assumes it. Here is what actually changes.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="checks">
      <li><span><b>Check the exclusion list before you plan around an offer</b>Google's student terms exclude six countries outright, Canada among them. That detail sits in support documentation rather than the announcement, which is exactly why it propagates through dozens of lists uncorrected.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>The tier you get depends on where your institution is</b>Google's offer gives U.S. students AI Pro and everyone else AI Plus. You do not get to choose, and the page reads the country set on your Google account.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Use a personal account, not your school Workspace one</b>If a student offer refuses you and your region is supported, a school-managed Google Workspace account is the usual cause. Switch to a personal address and retry.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Software perks travel better than consumer ones</b>Autodesk, MATLAB, JetBrains, Figma, GitHub and the cloud credits are broadly international. Streaming, retail and hardware discounts are the ones that thin out fastest outside the U.S.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Your library is the most reliable thing on this page</b>Institutional subscriptions do not care which country you are from — if you are enrolled, you have access on the same terms as everyone else in your programme.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 08 exit -->
  <section id="exit" class="sec">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">08</span>
      <h2>Before your email dies</h2>
      <p class="lede">Put this in your calendar for the start of your final semester. It is fifteen minutes of work that saves people years of files.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="checks">
      <li><span><b>Back up OneDrive first</b>The 1 TB of storage deactivates with your account. This is the single most common way students lose coursework, projects and photographs permanently.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Export from every cloud service you used</b>Figma files, Notion workspaces, cloud projects, MATLAB scripts, CAD models. Transfer ownership to a personal account where the service allows it.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Screenshot your licences and confirmations</b>If you ever need to prove you held a licence legitimately — and for anything you built commercially, you might — that record only exists in an inbox that is about to close.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Forward what matters off the school address</b>Account recovery emails, verification confirmations, anything tied to a service you intend to keep. Change the email on those accounts before, not after.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Check which perks survive</b>A few genuinely do. Notion's education plan has covered qualifying alumni domains, Prime Student converts to a discounted year rather than ending, Apple's education store stays open if you are continuing to a master's, and your public library card replaces a good chunk of the news access.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Claim the slow ones now, not later</b>Anything requiring document review — the GitHub pack, institutional software portals — takes days. Starting these in your final month is how people miss them entirely.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="k">The one-line version</span>
      <p>Every perk on this page requires an active student email at the moment you claim it. The email stops working before you expect it to — often within weeks of your final term, not on graduation day.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 09 register -->
  <section id="register" class="sec">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <span class="sec-num">09</span>
      <h2>What was checked, and where</h2>
      <p class="lede">Offers in this category change monthly, so every claim here carries a date. Where a fact came from a company's own documentation rather than a roundup, that is noted — and where it didn't, that's noted too.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Source type</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Google student offer relaunched 19 Aug 2026; U.S. gets AI Pro, 140+ markets get AI Plus; redeem by 31 Dec 2026</td><td>Google's own announcement</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Canada, Albania, Bolivia, Hong Kong, Macau, Tunisia excluded</td><td>Google One support documentation</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Azure for Students: $100 credit, 12 months, 25+ free services, cancels when exhausted</td><td>Microsoft Learn documentation</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GitHub Copilot student sign-ups paused Apr 2026, since reopened as a Student plan</td><td>Third-party reporting — verify on the page</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Perplexity referral-bonus programme ended 31 May 2026</td><td>Third-party reporting</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Student Pack contents and roughly 2-year validity</td><td>Third-party roundups, cross-checked</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Consumer pricing (Spotify, Prime, Apple, YouTube)</td><td>Third-party roundups; U.S. pricing</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Autodesk and MATLAB list values</td><td>Third-party estimates — treat as approximate</td><td class="num">30 Aug 2026</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout neutral">
      <span class="k">Be sceptical of the numbers, including mine</span>
      <p>Every dollar figure on this page is an approximation, most are U.S. pricing, and several are list values nobody actually pays. They are here to help you prioritise, not to be quoted. The claim links go to the official page in each case — that page, not this one, is the authority on what you can currently get.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="follow">
      <h3>This list gets maintained</h3>
      <p>Student offers rotate constantly — three of the ones above changed materially in 2026 alone. I re-check the page and update the register when they move.</p>
